<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ciliwung River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ciliwung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ciliwung is a river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, flowing north from the highlands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a>, the chief port of the Sunda Kingdom, lay at its mouth and served the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a> upriver. The later cities of Jayakarta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> grew on the same site, and the river still crosses Jakarta.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ciliwung-river.jpg" alt="Ciliwung River" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wibowo Djatmiko (Wie146) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciliwung_100303-0435_jkt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
